### How the pieces stay modular

* **Utility functions** (`mousePos`, `snapToGrid`, `snapToWireframe`) are pure helpers – they don’t touch global state.
* **Drawing** is split into `drawGrid`, `drawLines`, `redraw`, and `previewLine` (embedded in the mouse‑move handler).  
  Changing the grid size only requires updating `GRID_SIZE` and calling `redraw()`.
* **Persistence** lives in `saveLines` / `loadLines`. They are called only where needed (after a line is added, on start‑up, and on clear).
* **UI wiring** (dropdown, button, tooltip) is isolated at the bottom of the script, making it trivial to move the controls elsewhere or replace them with a framework component.

Drop the whole block into the page that already contains a `<canvas id="c">` (or keep the canvas tag as shown) and you’ll have a fully functional, state‑preserving snap‑to‑grid tool ready for the “Mapping Historic Franklin” project. Enjoy!

Below is a **single copy‑and‑paste HTML + JavaScript** file that contains everything you asked for:

* **Undo** – removes the most‑recent line and updates `localStorage`.  
* **Export CSV** – builds a CSV string (`x1,y1,x2,y2`) and forces a download.  
* **Responsive canvas** – the canvas always fills the width of its container; when the window is resized the canvas element is resized, the stored offsets are refreshed and the whole drawing (grid + all lines) is redrawn.  
* All the features from the previous version (grid‑size dropdown, clear button, tooltip, persistent storage) remain unchanged.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Snap‑to‑Grid / Wireframe Tool – Mapping Historic Franklin</title>
<style>
  /* ---- Layout --------------------------------------------------- */
  #canvasWrapper {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;               /* fill the container */
    max-width: 800px;          /* optional max width */
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  canvas {
    width: 100%;               /* CSS size – makes it responsive */
    height: auto;
    background:#fff;
    border:1px solid #999;
    display:block;
  }
  #tooltip {
    position:absolute;
    pointer-events:none;
    background:#fff;
    border:1px solid #333;
    padding:2px 4px;
    font-size:12px;
    white-space:nowrap;
    display:none;
    z-index:10;
  }
  #controls {
    margin-top:8px;
    text-align:center;
  }
  button, select {
    margin:0 4px;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="canvasWrapper">
  <canvas id="c"></canvas>
  <div id="tooltip"></div>
</div>

<div id="controls">
  <label for="gridSizeSelect">Grid size:</label>
  <select id="gridSizeSelect">
    <option value="20">20 px</option>
    <option value="40" selected>40 px</option>
    <option value="60">60 px</option>
  </select>

  <button id="clearBtn">Clear</button>
  <button id="undoBtn">Undo</button>
  <button id="exportBtn">Export CSV</button>
</div>

<script>
/* ==============================================================
   0️⃣  GLOBALS & STATE
   ============================================================== */
const canvas   = document.getElementById('c');
const ctx      = canvas.getContext('2d');
const tooltip  = document.getElementById('tooltip');
const STORAGE_KEY = 'snapLines';

let GRID_SIZE   = 40;                     // will be overwritten by dropdown
let canvasOffsets = canvas.getBoundingClientRect(); // fixes the ReferenceError
let isDrawing   = false;
let startPoint  = null;
const lines      = [];                    // {p1:{x,y}, p2:{x,y}}
const wireVertices = [                    // dummy 3‑D projection – replace with yours
  {x:100, y:100}, {x:300, y:100},
  {x:300, y:300}, {x:100, y:300},
  {x:150, y:150}, {x:250, y:150},
  {x:250, y:250}, {x:150, y:250}
];

/* ---- Keep offsets up‑to‑date on any page change ----------------- */
window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
  canvasOffsets = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
  resizeCanvas();               // also updates the canvas size
});

/* ==============================================================
   1️⃣  RESPONSIVE CANVAS
   ============================================================== */
const ORIGINAL_WIDTH  = 800;   // the size you designed the drawing for
const ORIGINAL_HEIGHT = 500;

function resizeCanvas() {
  const wrapper = document.getElementById('canvasWrapper');
  const newW = wrapper.clientWidth;               // fill the wrapper width
  const newH = Math.round(newW * ORIGINAL_HEIGHT / ORIGINAL_WIDTH);
  canvas.width  = newW;      // **attributes** – actual pixel buffer
  canvas.height = newH;
  // after changing size we must recompute offsets
  canvasOffsets = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
  redraw();                 // redraw everything at the new size
}

/* Initialise size once */
resizeCanvas();

/* ==============================================================
   2️⃣  MOUSE → CANVAS COORDINATES
   ============================================================== */
function mousePos(evt) {
  return {
    x: evt.clientX - canvasOffsets.left,
    y: evt.clientY - canvasOffsets.top
  };
}

/* ==============================================================
   3️⃣  GRID & SNAPPING HELPERS
   ============================================================== */
function snapToGrid(pt) {
  return {
    x: Math.round(pt.x / GRID_SIZE) * GRID_SIZE,
    y: Math.round(pt.y / GRID_SIZE) * GRID_SIZE
  };
}
function snapToWireframe(pt, tolerance = 15) {
  let best = null, bestDist = Infinity;
  for (const v of wireVertices) {
    const d = Math.hypot(v.x - pt.x, v.y - pt.y);
    if (d < bestDist && d <= tolerance) {
      bestDist = d;
      best = v;
    }
  }
  return best ? {x: best.x, y: best.y, snappedTo: 'wireframe'} : null;
}

/* ==============================================================
   4️⃣  PERSISTENCE (localStorage)
   ============================================================== */
function saveLines() {
  localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify(lines));
}
function loadLines() {
  const data = localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
  if (!data) return;
  try {
    const parsed = JSON.parse(data);
    if (Array.isArray(parsed)) lines.push(...parsed);
  } catch (e) {
    console.warn('Could not parse stored lines', e);
  }
}

/* ==============================================================
   5️⃣  DRAWING ROUTINES
   ============================================================== */
function drawGrid() {
  ctx.strokeStyle = '#e0e0e0';
  ctx.lineWidth   = 1;
  for (let x = 0; x <= canvas.width; x += GRID_SIZE) {
    ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(x, 0); ctx.lineTo(x, canvas.height); ctx.stroke();
  }
  for (let y = 0; y <= canvas.height; y += GRID_SIZE) {
    ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(0, y); ctx.lineTo(canvas.width, y); ctx.stroke();
  }
}
function drawLines() {
  ctx.strokeStyle = '#000';
  ctx.lineWidth   = 2;
  for (const ln of lines) {
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(ln.p1.x, ln.p1.y);
    ctx.lineTo(ln.p2.x, ln.p2.y);
    ctx.stroke();
  }
}
function redraw() {
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
  drawGrid();
  drawLines();
}

/* ==============================================================
   6️⃣  INTERACTION – CLICK / MOVE
   ============================================================== */
canvas.addEventListener('mousedown', evt => {
  const raw  = mousePos(evt);
  const grid = snapToGrid(raw);
  const wire = snapToWireframe(raw);
  const pt   = wire ? {...wire, snappedTo:'wireframe'} : {...grid, snappedTo:'grid'};

  if (!isDrawing) {
    startPoint = pt;
    isDrawing  = true;
  } else {
    const endPoint = pt;
    lines.push({p1:startPoint, p2:endPoint});
    saveLines();
    isDrawing = false;
    startPoint = null;
    redraw();
  }
});

canvas.addEventListener('mousemove', evt => {
  const raw = mousePos(evt);
  const grid = snapToGrid(raw);
  const wire = snapToWireframe(raw);
  const curPt = wire ? {...wire, snappedTo:'wireframe'} : {...grid, snappedTo:'grid'};

  // ---- preview while dragging ----
  if (isDrawing) {
    redraw();
    ctx.strokeStyle = 'rgba(0,0,255,0.5)';
    ctx.lineWidth   = 1;
    ctx.setLineDash([5,5]);
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(startPoint.x, startPoint.y);
    ctx.lineTo(curPt.x, curPt.y);
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.setLineDash([]);
  }

  // ---- tooltip ----
  const tolerance = 10; // px tolerance for “hovering” a snap point
  const distToSnap = Math.hypot(curPt.x - raw.x, curPt.y - raw.y);
  if (distToSnap <= tolerance) {
    tooltip.style.left = (evt.pageX + 12) + 'px';
    tooltip.style.top  = (evt.pageY + 12) + 'px';
    tooltip.textContent = `(${curPt.x}, ${curPt.y})`;
    tooltip.style.display = 'block';
  } else {
    tooltip.style.display = 'none';
  }
});

/* ==============================================================
   7️⃣  UI CONTROLS
   ============================================================== */
// ---- grid‑size dropdown ----
document.getElementById('gridSizeSelect').addEventListener('change', e => {
  GRID_SIZE = Number(e.target.value);
  redraw();
});

// ---- clear button ----
document.getElementById('clearBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  lines.length = 0;
  localStorage.removeItem(STORAGE_KEY);
  redraw();
});

// ---- undo button ----
document.getElementById('undoBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  if (lines.length) {
    lines.pop();               // remove last line
    saveLines();               // update storage
    redraw();
  }
});

// ---- export CSV button ----
document.getElementById('exportBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  if (!lines.length) return alert('No lines to export.');
  const header = 'x1,y1,x2,y2';
  const rows = lines.map(l => `${l.p1.x},${l.p1.y},${l.p2.x},${l.p2.y}`);
  const csvContent = [header, ...rows].join('\n');
  const blob = new Blob([csvContent], {type:'text/csv'});
  const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
  const a = document.createElement('a');
  a.href = url;
  a.download = 'lines.csv';
  a.click();
  URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
});

/* ==============================================================
   8️⃣  INITIALISATION
   ============================================================== */
loadLines();               // restore any saved lines
if (lines.length) redraw(); // draw them (grid is drawn inside redraw)
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How the **responsive canvas** works

1. **CSS** gives the `<canvas>` a `width:100%` so it always stretches to the width of its parent (`#canvasWrapper`).  
2. The **`resizeCanvas()`** function (called once on load and again on every `window.resize`) reads the wrapper’s current width, computes a height that preserves the original aspect ratio (`ORIGINAL_HEIGHT / ORIGINAL_WIDTH`), and **sets the canvas *attributes*** `canvas.width` and `canvas.height` to those pixel values.  
3. Changing the canvas’s *attributes* clears its bitmap, so we immediately call `redraw()` which:
   * recomputes the canvas offsets,
   * draws the grid with the new spacing,
   * re‑draws every line stored in the `lines` array.
4. Because the logical coordinates of every line are kept in the same pixel space (they are not scaled), the visual result looks exactly the same after a resize — only the canvas element itself got larger or smaller.

All other features (grid‑size dropdown, clear/undo/export, tooltip, persistence) remain untouched. Feel free to replace the dummy `wireVertices` with your real 3‑D projection data, tweak the aspect‑ratio constants, or style the controls to match the look of the “Mapping Historic Franklin” site. Happy mapping!

Sure! In modern Python (3.5+), you can merge two dictionaries with the `{**a, **b}` unpacking syntax, or with the `|` operator starting in Python 3.9.

```python
# Two sample dictionaries
dict_a = {"apple": 1, "banana": 2}
dict_b = {"banana": 3, "cherry": 4}

# Method 1: dictionary unpacking (Python 3.5+)
merged = {**dict_a, **dict_b}
print(merged)   # {'apple': 1, 'banana': 3, 'cherry': 4}

# Method 2: merge operator (Python 3.9+)
merged2 = dict_a | dict_b
print(merged2)  # {'apple': 1, 'banana': 3, 'cherry': 4}
```

**What happens with overlapping keys?**  
Both approaches let the later dictionary (`dict_b`) overwrite values from the earlier one (`dict_a`). In the example, the key `"banana"` ends up with the value `3`.

If you need to keep both values for overlapping keys, you’d have to handle that manually (e.g., store them in a list). But for a straightforward concatenation, the two snippets above are clean and idiomatic.

