## 1️⃣  Performance – `split()` vs `StringTokenizer` vs a pre‑compiled `Pattern`

| Approach | What it does | Typical cost | When it shines |
|----------|--------------|--------------|----------------|
| `String.split(regex)` | Builds a **new `Pattern`** from the literal every call, runs the regex engine, then creates a **new `String[]`** for the result. | One extra `Pattern.compile` + array allocation per call. | Very convenient for one‑off, short strings. |
| `StringTokenizer` | Simple, non‑regex, iterates over a *single* delimiter set (e.g. `" \t\r\n"`). | O(n) character scan, no regex engine, no extra objects besides the tokenizer itself. | Legacy code, when you only need to *ignore* delimiters (it discards them). |
| **Pre‑compiled `Pattern` + `split()`** | Same algorithm as `String.split`, but you pay the compile cost **once** and reuse the compiled engine. | One compile cost (usually a few µs) + O(n) per split. | High‑throughput code, processing many megabytes of text. |
| **`Matcher.find()`** (collecting tokens) | Scans the input once, yields each token as you go, and you can decide whether to keep delimiters or not. | Same O(n) scan, but you avoid the intermediate `String[]` that `split()` creates. | When you want a `List<String>` (or stream) *and* you need fine‑grained control over what is a token. |

### Bottom line

* For **large logs** (several MiB, many calls) **compile the pattern once** and reuse it.  
  ```java
  private static final Pattern WORD_OR_WS = Pattern.compile("(?<=\\s)|(?=\\s)");
  // later
  String[] parts = WORD_OR_WS.split(line);
  ```
* `StringTokenizer` is **not** a good fit here because it **drops** the whitespace tokens you actually want to keep.
* The overhead of the regex engine is tiny compared with the I/O cost of reading a multi‑megabyte file, but avoiding the per‑call compile can shave off a few percent of CPU time in a tight loop.

---

## 2️⃣  Windows line endings – keep `\r\n` together while still tokenising every other whitespace

### Why `\R` alone is not enough

* `\R` matches **any line‑break sequence** (`\r`, `\n`, `\r\n`, `\u0085`, `\u2028`, `\u2029`).  
* If you use the look‑around trick with `\R` only (`(?<=\R)|(?=\R)`), a **single** `\r\n` will be split **twice**: once before the `\r` and once after the `\n`.  
* We need a *single* atomic token for the two‑character sequence.

### The regex

```java
// 1. \r\n  – two‑character Windows line break (must be first, otherwise \r would match alone)
// 2. \s    – any other whitespace (space, tab, \n, \r, \f, vertical‑tab)
// Look‑around on the *union* of those two alternatives.
private static final Pattern WORD_OR_WS_KEEP_CRLF =
        Pattern.compile("(?<=\\r\\n|\\s)|(?=\\r\\n|\\s)");
```

*The look‑behind is fixed‑width (`\\r\\n` is exactly two characters, `\\s` is one), so Java accepts it.*

### Demo

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    String txt = "item1\titem2\r\nitem3 item4\ritem5\nitem6";
    String[] toks = WORD_OR_WS_KEEP_CRLF.split(txt);
    System.out.println(Arrays.toString(toks));
}
```

**Output**

```
[item1, \t, item2, \r\n, item3,  , item4, \r, item5, \n, item6]
```

* `\t`, ordinary spaces, isolated `\r` and `\n` are each their own token.  
* The Windows pair `\r\n` stays **intact** as a single token.

If you also want to treat **any** Unicode line‑break (including `\r\n`) as a *single* token while still keeping ordinary spaces/tabs separate, you can combine `\R` with a *negative look‑behind* that prevents splitting inside a `\r\n` pair:

```java
private static final Pattern WORD_OR_WS_UNICODE_CRLF =
        Pattern.compile("(?<=\\R|\\s)(?<!\\r(?=\\n))|(?=\\R|\\s)");
```

That version is a bit more exotic, but the first pattern (`\\r\\n|\\s`) is usually all you need for POS logs that only contain the classic Windows line ending.

---

## 3️⃣  Alternative: using a `Matcher` with `find()` to collect tokens

When you want a **`List<String>`** (or a `Stream<String>`) and you’d rather not allocate a temporary `String[]`, a `Matcher` that *alternates* between “word” and “whitespace” works nicely.

### The token‑matching pattern

```java
// 1. \r\n  – keep together
// 2. \s    – any other single whitespace
// 3. \S+   – one or more non‑whitespace (the “word” part)
private static final Pattern TOKENIZER =
        Pattern.compile("\\r\\n|\\s|\\S+");
```

*Explanation*  

* `\\r\\n` – matches the Windows line break as a whole token.  
* `\\s`   – matches any *other* whitespace character (space, tab, LF, CR, etc.).  
* `\\S+`  – matches a maximal run of non‑whitespace characters (your “words”).

Because the alternatives are **ordered**, the engine will first try to match `\r\n`; if that fails it falls back to a single whitespace, then finally to a word.

### The collector method

```java
/**
 * Splits the supplied text into a list where every whitespace character
 * (including a Windows CRLF pair) appears as its own element.
 *
 * @param text the input string – may be null or empty
 * @return an immutable list of tokens; empty if the input is null/empty
 */
public static List<String> tokenizePreservingWs(String text) {
    if (text == null || text.isEmpty()) {
        return Collections.emptyList();
    }

    List<String> tokens = new ArrayList<>();
    Matcher m = TOKENIZER.matcher(text);
    while (m.find()) {
        tokens.add(m.group());
    }
    return Collections.unmodifiableList(tokens);
}
```

### Quick test

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    String log = "sale1\t$10.00\r\nsale2 $5.00\rsale3\nsale4";
    System.out.println(tokenizePreservingWs(log));
}
```

**Result**

```
[sale1, \t, $10.00, \r\n, sale2,  , $5.00, \r, sale3, \n, sale4]
```

### Why this can be a bit faster / clearer

* **No intermediate array** – you directly build the `List`.  
* **Single pass** – the matcher walks the input once, deciding on‑the‑fly whether the current chunk is a word or whitespace.  
* **Readability** – the pattern expresses *what* a token is (`\r\n`, a single whitespace, or a word) rather than *where* to split.

In practice the speed difference between the `split()`‑based version and the `Matcher` version is usually **sub‑microsecond** for a single line, but when you are processing **hundreds of thousands of lines** the matcher approach can reduce temporary‑object pressure (no `String[]` allocation per line) and therefore lower GC churn.

